The set \(S\) must have no three consecutive integers, otherwise it would have zero cousins. The number of cousins equals the number of injective assignments \(f:S\to\mathbb Z\setminus S\) with \(f(s)\in\{s-1,s+1\}\). This number factors as a product over connected components of the index graph induced by gaps of size 1. For a component of size \(m\) (number of elements), the number of assignments is \(1\) if at least one of the component's endpoints is forced to a unique choice, and \(m+1\) if both endpoints are free. To obtain many cousins and minimize \(|S|\), we want all components to have the free-end condition, giving a factor \(m_i+1\) for a component of size \(m_i\). Thus the total number of cousins is \(\prod_i (m_i+1)=4040\) and we want to minimize \(\sum_i m_i = \sum_i ((m_i+1)-1) = \bigl(\sum_i (m_i+1)\bigr) - k\), where \(k\) is the number of components.

Minimizing \(\sum m_i\) is equivalent to minimizing \(\sum (m_i+1)\) for a fixed product. The prime factorization of \(4040\) is \(2^3\cdot5\cdot101\). Taking each prime factor as a separate component (i.e., \(m_i+1 = 2,2,2,5,101\)) gives \(\sum (m_i+1)=2+2+2+5+101=112\) and \(k=5\), so \(\sum m_i =112-5=107\). Any other factorization into factors \(\ge 2\) yields a larger sum (e.g., \(4,2,5,101\) gives sum \(112\) with \(k=4\) → \(\sum m_i=108\); \(5,8,101\) gives sum \(114\) with \(k=3\) → \(\sum m_i=111\)). Hence the minimal possible number of elements is \(107\).

\boxed{107}

